<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Transparency Problem</div>
<h2>A Model That Thinks Partly in Float Vectors</h2>
<p>Autoregressive models like Gemma 4 reason entirely in natural language: every token of "thinking" passes through human-readable text before the next token is generated. This makes chain-of-thought remarkably auditable — if a model misbehaves, the reasoning is right there to read.</p>
<p>DiffusionGemma breaks this assumption. It generates a full <em>canvas</em> of C tokens by running T denoising steps, each consisting of a transformer forward pass and a sampling step. Between each denoising step, two things pass forward: the canvas tokens <strong>o</strong> (readable) and a self-conditioning matrix <strong>S</strong> ∈ ℝ<sup>C×d_model</sup> (dense float vectors, not readable). This matrix encodes what the model is "thinking" about each token position without ever forming words.</p>
<p>The question this paper asks is direct: is DiffusionGemma less transparent than Gemma 4 because of this? The answer is: <em>it depends on whether you can read those intermediate vectors</em> — and the paper shows you can.</p>

<div class="vp">
