<p>The self-conditioning matrix <strong>S<sup>t</sup></strong> is not random noise — it is computed as a soft embedding of the logit distribution: <code>S^t = softmax(ℓ̂/τ) · W_E</code>. This means the information in S<sup>t</sup> is already biased toward token embedding directions. The question is whether that bias is strong enough to read it back.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-depth" data-code="depth">
<div class="section-no">02 — Opaque Serial Depth</div>
<h2>How Much Hidden Computation Is There?</h2>
<p>The paper formalizes transparency using <strong>opaque serial depth</strong>: treat the model's computation as a Boolean circuit, mark the interpretable nodes (human-readable text tokens), and find the longest path through that circuit without hitting an interpretable node. Think of it as "how long is the longest unmonitored chain of reasoning?"</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Opaque serial depth comparison (empirical upper bounds, 256k context)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click bars to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="depth-canvas" style="width:100%;height:180px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.5rem;margin-top:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:2px;background:rgba(139,92,246,.5);display:inline-block"></span> Gemma 4 26B A4B (baseline AR)</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:2px;background:rgba(16,185,129,.5);display:inline-block"></span> DiffusionGemma + interpretable bottleneck</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:2px;background:rgba(239,68,68,.5);display:inline-block"></span> DiffusionGemma + opaque bottleneck (worst case)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The numbers are stark. Without any assumptions about S<sup>t</sup>, the model can chain 608,016 unmonitored computation steps — 28.6× Gemma 4's 21,235. This is the asymptotically natural T×L×(log N + log D + log C) scaling: 48 denoising steps multiply the serial depth. But if the intermediate bottleneck tokens are interpretable — which the next section establishes — the depth collapses to 23,571, just 1.1× Gemma.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Opaque Serial Depth Asymptotic Formula</div>
  <span class="math-var">Gemma 4</span>: O(L · (log N + log D))<br>
  <span class="math-var">DiffusionGemma</span> [interpretable bottleneck]: O(L · (log N + log D + log C))<br>
  <span class="math-var">DiffusionGemma</span> [opaque bottleneck]: O(T · L · (log N + log D + log C))<br>
  <span class="math-comment">T=48 denoising steps | L=layers | N=context tokens | C=canvas size | D=activation dim</span>
</div>

<p>In practice, DiffusionGemma uses adaptive stopping: it terminates early when token probabilities stop changing, typically using 12–16 steps rather than the full 48. The actual runtime depth may be roughly a third of the worst case.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Interpretable Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Top-k Tokens Cost Nothing</h2>
<p>To test whether S<sup>t</sup> can be made interpretable, the authors run ablations: replace the full self-conditioning matrix with a <em>top-k projection</em> at every denoising step and every token position. If k=8 out of 256,000 vocabulary entries captures all the signal, the matrix is effectively readable.</p>

<p>The projection function <code>f_k</code> keeps the logit mass of the k most probable tokens unchanged while setting all remaining logits to a uniform low value (so their softmax probabilities stay proportional among themselves). The resulting S'<sup>t</sup> is then re-embedded: <code>S'^t = W_E · softmax(f_k(ℓ̂))</code>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Capability benchmarks under top-k bottleneck ablation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a k value to explore</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" id="ablation-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:1rem">
      <button class="abl-btn active" data-k="1">k=1</button>
      <button class="abl-btn" data-k="2">k=2</button>
      <button class="abl-btn" data-k="4">k=4</button>
      <button class="abl-btn active-main" data-k="8">k=8 ✓</button>
      <button class="abl-btn" data-k="16">k=16</button>
      <button class="abl-btn" data-k="32">k=32</button>
      <button class="abl-btn" data-k="64">k=64</button>
      <button class="abl-btn" data-k="baseline">baseline</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ablation-chart" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem"></div>
    <div id="ablation-verdict" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>At k=8 — just 8 tokens out of a vocabulary of ~256k — performance is indistinguishable from the full self-conditioning baseline across all four benchmarks: Natural2Code, LiveCodeBench, AMC/AIME, and GPQA. The same is true for a probability threshold of p=0.03. This means the self-conditioning matrix carries no useful information beyond what can be expressed by a handful of token probabilities.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key insight:</strong> Restricting S<sup>t</sup> to 8 tokens <em>reduces the opaque serial depth by a factor of 26</em> (from 48 denoising steps to approximately 2). The question becomes: are these 8 tokens actually interpretable to a human reader?
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-tokens" data-code="tokens">
<div class="section-no">04 — What Are the Intermediate Tokens?</div>
<h2>Reading the Bottleneck: 85%+ Interpretable From Step 0</h2>
<p>Having shown the bottleneck can be compressed to ~8 tokens without cost, the authors next ask whether those tokens are meaningful. They run 800 WildChat prompts, extract the top-8 and p&gt;0.03 tokens at every denoising step, and classify each token into five categories:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Final Token</strong> — exactly matches the token the model will output at this position</li>
  <li><strong>Adjacent Token</strong> — matches a final token at a nearby position (±10)</li>
  <li><strong>Similar to Final</strong> — among the 50 nearest neighbors by cosine similarity to the final token</li>
  <li><strong>Similar to Adjacent</strong> — semantically similar to a nearby final token</li>
  <li><strong>Other</strong> — uninterpretable — doesn't fit any category above</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Intermediate token category breakdown across denoising steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle filter to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
      <button class="filter-btn active" data-filter="p03">p &gt; 0.03 filter</button>
      <button class="filter-btn" data-filter="topk">Top K = 8 filter</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="category-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;margin-top:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:2px;background:#8b5cf6;display:inline-block"></span> Final Token</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:2px;background:#06b6d4;display:inline-block"></span> Adjacent Token</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:2px;background:#10b981;display:inline-block"></span> Similar (final/adj)</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:2px;background:rgba(239,68,68,.8);display:inline-block"></span> Other (opaque)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>With the p&gt;0.03 threshold, over 85% of tokens are interpretable <em>from the very first denoising step</em>. The model is already drafting mostly-readable guesses about its final output from step 0. The "Other" category — truly opaque tokens — is small and shrinks further as denoising progresses.</p>

<p>The Top-K=8 filter tells a different story: late-step "Other" tokens spike. This happens because at later steps, the model becomes very confident in a single token — so the other 7 slots in the top-8 capture low-probability noise, not genuine reasoning. The p&gt;0.03 filter naturally adapts: as the model becomes confident, fewer tokens meet the threshold, and you're left with only the genuinely meaningful predictions.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-reasoning" data-code="reasoning">
<div class="section-no">05 — Non-Autoregressive Reasoning</div>
<h2>What Autoregressive Models Can't Do</h2>
<p>Even with a largely interpretable bottleneck, DiffusionGemma reasons differently from autoregressive models. Because all canvas positions can be updated at every denoising step, the model can write tokens in any order — and it exploits this freedom in surprisingly structured ways. The paper documents four distinct phenomena.</p>

<h3>Retroactive Self-Correction</h3>
<p>An autoregressive model answering "How many perfect squares are between 400 and 800?" must commit to an answer before writing its reasoning. DiffusionGemma can write a guess, fill in the reasoning, and then change the guess retroactively when the reasoning forces a different answer. Click through the steps below:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Retroactive self-correction — "squares between 400 and 800"</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the denoising process</span>
  </div>
