<h3>Early Length Prediction</h3>
<p>On a prompt like "Explain photosynthesis in 2 sentences", DiffusionGemma predicts padding tokens at positions beyond ~55 with near-100% confidence <em>after just the first denoising step</em> — before it has decided what words to write. The final response converges to 50 tokens, close to the early prediction. The model knows how long it will write before knowing what it will say.</p>

<h3>Token Smearing</h3>
<p>When writing a function to classify balanced brackets, the word "bracket" must appear in the docstring — but the model doesn't know exactly where. It spreads probability mass for "bracket" across a cluster of adjacent positions, then locks it in once the surrounding context is settled. This is only possible because every position can be revised at each step.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Token smearing — "bracket" spread before converging</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">early step: probability smeared; late step: locked in</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="smear-canvas" style="width:100%;height:120px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Intermediate Context Reasoning</h3>
<p>On a Fibonacci-with-replacement task — "compute the sequence but replace every '3' with the word 'Gold'" — DiffusionGemma can first generate the digit 3 as an intermediate token (to use for computing subsequent terms), and then replace it with "Gold" in a later denoising step. The digit 3 is causally necessary for the correct answer but never appears in the final output. Gemini 2.5 Pro cannot do this task without chain-of-thought reasoning.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-monitor" data-code="monitor">
<div class="section-no">06 — Monitorability Results</div>
<h2>Similarly Monitorable to Gemma 4</h2>
<p>The paper's main practical finding: despite the architectural differences and the non-autoregressive reasoning behaviors, DiffusionGemma is <strong>as monitorable as Gemma 4</strong> on a comprehensive suite of safety-relevant evaluations adapted from Guan et al. (2025).</p>
<p>Monitorability is measured via G-mean² of a binary classifier: can a monitor model — given the full response (CoT + output) — correctly identify whether the model misbehaved? Higher is better. The evaluations cover three categories:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Intervention</strong> — does the chain of thought reflect a change in behavior when the input is perturbed?</li>
  <li><strong>Process</strong> — can a monitor detect which solution path (e.g. which theorem) the model used?</li>
  <li><strong>Outcome-property</strong> — can a monitor infer that the model exploited a structural property of the task (e.g. subverted unit tests)?</li>
</ul>

