<p>One confounding factor: DiffusionGemma generates significantly shorter chains of thought than Gemma 4 across all evaluations (e.g. 26,882 vs 30,005 average characters on DAFAT). Longer CoT generally correlates with higher monitorability, so DiffusionGemma's similar scores despite shorter thinking are notable — it may be conveying more information per character.</p>

<p>The paper's overall conclusion is cautiously optimistic: DiffusionGemma retains most of the transparency of autoregressive CoT today. But this relies on specific architectural choices — in particular, the projection that biases S<sup>t</sup> toward token embedding directions. Future diffusion models trained differently, or with more latent RL, might not maintain this property. The authors explicitly call for similar transparency audits to accompany future latent reasoning model releases.</p>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: How Transparent is DiffusionGemma?</h2>
    <p>DiffusionGemma from Google DeepMind generates text by running 48 denoising steps over a full canvas of tokens. Between each step, it passes both readable token predictions and an opaque self-conditioning matrix of dense float vectors. Naively, this gives it 28.6 times more "hidden computation" than Gemma 4 — but the paper shows this can be fixed by projecting the matrix through a token bottleneck. Using just the top 8 most probable tokens per position costs zero performance and reduces the opacity to just 1.1 times that of Gemma 4.</p>
    <p>More than 85 percent of intermediate tokens are interpretable from the very first denoising step — they are mostly guesses for the final output at that position or nearby positions. The paper also documents novel reasoning behaviors impossible for autoregressive models: retroactive self-correction, where the model writes a wrong answer and then corrects it after filling in reasoning; token smearing, where probability mass for a single token spreads across adjacent positions; and intermediate-context reasoning, where tokens are generated and then erased, never appearing in the final output. Despite all this, DiffusionGemma is as monitorable as Gemma 4 on safety-relevant evaluations, scoring 0.78 versus 0.82 G-mean squared on average.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：DiffusionGemma的透明度如何？</h2>
    <p>Google DeepMind的DiffusionGemma通过对完整token画布进行48步去噪来生成文本。在每步之间，模型传递可读的token预测和一个不透明的自条件矩阵（密集浮点向量）。若不对该矩阵做处理，模型的"隐式计算深度"比Gemma 4高28.6倍——但论文证明这可以通过将矩阵投影到token瓶颈来解决。每个位置仅保留概率最高的8个token，性能完全不受影响，透明度降至仅比Gemma 4高1.1倍。</p>
    <p>从第一步去噪开始，超过85%的中间token都是可解释的——它们大多是对当前或相邻位置最终输出的猜测。论文还记录了自回归模型无法实现的新型推理行为：逆向自我纠正（模型先写出错误答案，填写推理过程后再行纠正）、token涂抹（单个token的概率分散到相邻位置）以及中间上下文推理（生成后被删除、不出现在最终输出中的token）。尽管如此，DiffusionGemma在安全相关评估中的可监控性与Gemma 4相当，平均G均方分别为0.78和0.82。</p>
  </div>
</div>

</section>

